  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/22-spiny-eels/macrognathus_aral_en/",
      "html": "<p>In their natural habitat this species can reach a length of 65 cm, although in the fish tank it might not reach this length. Spiny eels make suitable and very interesting subjects for the home aquarium.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/22-spiny-eels/macrognathus_aral_en/",
      "html": "<p>This spiny eel originates from South East Asia, i.e. Pakistan, India, Sri Lanka, Bangladesh, Nepal up to Myanmar in their most southern distribution area.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/22-spiny-eels/macrognathus_aral_en/",
      "html": "<p>The water of the aquarium should be given some sea salt (1-2 tea spoons per 10 litre of water) and should be well aerated.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/22-spiny-eels/macrognathus_aral_en/",
      "html": "<p>This fish is dawn and night active and will eat small fish. Therefore it should only be kept with larger tank fellows. After a period of shyness the spiny eels will come out at feeding times and can even become a very trusty tank companion.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/22-spiny-eels/macrognathus_aral_en/",
      "html": "<p>Publications about breeding the spiny eels can be found in the literature of the last 40 years only incidentally, and only from the smaller species.</p>"
    }
  ],
